## Merge Sort

## Approach
-- Divide the array into two halves using mid.
-- Recursively divide each half until it contains only one element.
-- Merge the two sorted halves by comparing their elements.
-- Use a temporary array to store the sorted elements during merging.
-- Copy the elements from the temporary array back into the original array from low to high.
-- Since the array is divided into halves, there are log n levels, and at every level all n elements are processed during merging.

## Complexity
Time Complexity: O(n log n) — Best, Average, Worst
Space Complexity: O(n) — Temporary array used for merging.

SOLUTION :
// Recursion function to Divide the array into halfs till no one element able to divide
static void mergeSort(int[] arr, int low, int high) {
        if (low >= high) {
            return;
        }
        int mid = (low+high) /2;
        mergeSort(arr, low, mid);
        mergeSort(arr, mid+1, high);
        merge(arr, low, mid, high);
    }

// Recursion function to merge the elements with sorted order
    static void merge(int[] arr, int low, int mid, int high) {
        int left = low;
        int right = mid+1;
        int index = 0;
        int[] temp = new int[high - low + 1];
        while (left <= mid && right <= high) {
            if (arr[left] <= arr[right]) {
                temp[index] = arr[left];
                left++;
                index++;
            } else {
                temp[index] = arr[right];
                right++;
                index++;
            }
        }
        while (left <= mid) {
            temp[index] = arr[left];
            left++;
            index++;
        }
        while (right <= high) {
            temp[index] = arr[right];
            right++;
            index++;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < temp.length; i++) {
            arr[low + i] = temp[index];
        }
    }
